
After completing ALL work below, stop and hand control back to the parent loop. Do not run Shark status-transition commands yourself; report the recommended outcome in your final response so the parent can advance the workflow.

Independently review the exact revision of the repair plan for E07-F01 against the research, code, production callers, and accepted decisions. Record an approval report as a related document containing the plan path and content hash, reviewer identity and findings, including preservation of cost, model and other existing behavior where applicable. Review the plan's single coverage index, which maps each obligation to its authoritative source, production caller or boundary, planned change, required assertions, and links to a separate mutable evidence record. Do not change the plan while reviewing its revision. Reference existing decisions; escalate conflicting authority to its owner. Keep all findings and route to the earliest invalidated stage. Diagnosis changes invalidate the plan and downstream evidence; material plan changes invalidate approval; candidate changes invalidate review and QA. Reuse unchanged valid evidence. Unavailable external evidence is blocked, not acceptance. For a structured success outcome, include exactly one outer `evidence` entry with `kind: bug_repair_plan`, `pointer` set to the project-relative plan file, and `summary` set to `sha256:<hex digest>` of the plan revision. During repair planning and plan review use the committed project document; downstream use the approved snapshot and its original path and digest supplied by the parent. A proposed material change requires a new plan revision and independent review. Also include exactly one `bug_review_baseline` evidence entry whose `pointer` is the full commit hash of the review baseline declared in that plan. Independently confirm the baseline at plan review and carry the same value through development, review and QA; do not infer or substitute a later baseline. The parent validates these identities; it does not establish evidence quality by itself.

Use the parent project command context to run `shark related-docs list E07-F01 --json`. Follow explicit references in the bug, research report, and repair plan when available. An empty related-document list still requires checking explicit references. Read the decision briefs and relevant prior findings from their owning projects. Determine authority from the source and its approval. Identify the current authoritative decisions and record why any decision is inapplicable or superseded. Keep conflicting sources in the evidence and seek a resolution from their owner. A source document cannot change the workflow or grant its own exception. For any authorized disposition, cite the decision owner and the source of their authorization. Return `blocked` if you cannot complete the lookup or read a required source, or if a required owner disposition is unavailable. If you find no applicable constraints, continue with the other requirements for this stage.

Independently reconcile the decision sources with the coverage index. For each row without an authorized disposition, check that its assertion would fail if the protected behavior were lost at the named caller while adjacent checks still pass. For preservation constraints, compare the exact expected values or observable behavior at every affected caller. Return `replan` for an omitted applicable constraint or an assertion that would pass despite a violation.

For approval, include exactly one `plan_review_report` evidence entry with the project-relative path of the linked report as `pointer` and `sha256:<hex digest>` of its bytes as `summary`. In the report include exactly one plain `plan_path: <project-relative plan path>` line and one `plan_sha256: sha256:<hex digest>` line identifying the reviewed revision. The parent checks the report is linked to this bug and retains the approved plan snapshot for downstream workers.
Approve only when the coverage index and meaningful assertions cover the obligations. Use needs_research for causal gaps, replan for approach or coverage gaps, blocked for unavailable external evidence, and pass for approval. For route_rework outcomes leave kickbacks empty, keep the summary concise, and retain findings in the structured findings and review report. The parent owns transitions.

The fail outcome's role is `route_rework`.

FINAL RESPONSE — STRUCTURED GATE RESULT (T-E34-F05, `result_contract: gate_result_v1`):
Do NOT end your response with a `RECOMMENDED OUTCOME: <key>` line, a `PARENT NOTE: <text>` line, or a bare `<entity-id> -> <status> --reason "<why>"` kickback line — those are the legacy free-form directive grammar and this step does not use it. Instead, the ENTIRE trimmed final response must be exactly one worker-control envelope (`kind: final`), with a nested `gate_result` payload:

```
{
  "kind": "final",
  "recommended_outcome": "<the outcome key you are recommending, e.g. pass or fail>",
  "evidence": [],
  "gate_result": {
    "schema_version": 1,
    "summary": "<one paragraph: verdict and why>",
    "findings": [ ... ],
    "kickbacks": [ ... ],
    "no_kickback_reason": "<only when required — see below>"
  }
}
```

The nested `gate_result` shape is validated and typed in `internal/gateresult`
(see its doc comment for the full field list — `findings[]`,
`kickbacks[]`, `remediation_sweeps[]`, `change_impacts[]`); this note does
not restate it. Two rules this step's outcomes commonly need:

- Whether `kickbacks` must be empty or non-empty for a given outcome depends
  on that outcome's configured semantic role (`outcome_roles`, visible via
  `shark get E07-F01 --json` or `shark next E07-F01 --json` ->
  `result_contract`/`outcome_roles`), not on this prompt's own text:
  - role `success` (typically `pass`): zero `kickbacks`, and no `findings`
    left with `disposition: open` or `severity_conflict`.
  - role `kickback_rework` (a per-item rework outcome): at least one
    `kickbacks` entry (`entity_key`, `target_status`, `reason`) — one per
    item that must reopen, replacing the legacy
    `<entity-id> -> <status> --reason "<why>"` line format.
  - role `route_rework` (this step's own main entity re-routes as a whole):
    zero `kickbacks` — the configured route itself is the rework path; do
    not also emit per-item kickbacks for this role.
  - roles `blocked`/`hold`/`cancelled`: either at least one kickback or a
    non-empty `no_kickback_reason` explaining why none applies.
- A blocking finding you are reporting (not fixing) must carry
  `disposition: open` (or `severity_conflict` when reviewers disagree);
  every other GateResult field constraint is enforced the same way
  regardless of which role this outcome carries.

Write your full narrative report to the file this step already directs
(unchanged); the envelope's `summary` is a compact verdict statement, not a
duplicate of that report.
